{
  "title": "EUREKA: Human-Level Reward Design via Coding Large Language Models",
  "authors": [
    "Yecheng Jason Ma",
    "William Liang",
    "Guanzhi Wang",
    "De-An Huang",
    "Osbert Bastani",
    "Dinesh Jayaraman",
    "Yuke Zhu",
    "Linxi \"Jim\" Fan",
    "Anima Anandkumar"
  ],
  "year": 2024,
  "arxivId": "2310.12931",
  "abstract": "EUREKA is a novel reward design algorithm that leverages coding large language models to generate human-level rewards for a wide range of robotics tasks. By combining evolutionary search and in-context learning, EUREKA can autonomously generate rewards that outperform expert human-engineered rewards in 83% of tasks, leading to an average normalized improvement of 52%. This breakthrough has significant implications for the field of robotics and reinforcement learning.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "Imagine you're trying to teach a robot to perform a complex task, like spinning a pen. <strong> Reward design </strong> is the process of defining a reward function that encourages the robot to learn the desired behavior. However, designing effective rewards can be challenging, even for human experts. EUREKA addresses this problem by using <strong> coding large language models </strong> to generate rewards. These models can write code and improve it through <strong> evolutionary search </strong> and <strong> in-context learning </strong>.",
